# Stage 1 — Brief & grounding

Every round starts from Will's words, not from a plan of ours.

1. Capture the direction VERBATIM. Will's notes for a round are
   short and dense; keep his exact words in the record (the task
   register entry carries them). Round 2's seven notes and Round
   3's eight steps both survive verbatim in their briefs — see
   1.1.1/3.3.1/1.1.1 and 1.1.1/3.3.2/1.1.1.
2. Compute the task's reference coordinate from the request's
   exact words (Singular holographic merge) and record the task at
   that coordinate. The coordinate rides with the work from then
   on.
3. Write a BRIEF: a file, not a chat message. The brief restates
   the direction as production instructions, names the sources
   (which script, which demo build, which logs), and states the
   constraints carried from earlier rounds. The brief governs the
   build; workers read the brief, not the chat.
4. Make grounding calls explicitly, and FLAG them for overrule.
   Where Will's words need interpretation, decide, write the
   decision in the brief, and surface it to Will in the delivery
   message as a call he can reverse. Worked examples:
   - "Use real Wikipedia articles, or write full entries" →
     decided: full ORIGINAL entries written to a real standard
     (bulk-lifting Wikipedia's text sits badly with Will's
     ends-never-justify-the-means standard). Flagged; not overruled.
   - Music: the production notes named licensed routes (Artlist /
     Epidemic) but the round-3 message authorized no purchase →
     decided: original in-house bed this pass, licensed swap
     flagged as Will's call. No purchase was made.
   - The scale claims (trillions of users, shard-friendly) →
     carried as DESIGN INTENT in Will's voice, labelled as such
     in the script's claim appendix, never dressed as a measured
     benchmark.
5. Dispatch the build as one pipeline to a coordinator worker
   with the brief as its governing document: script → demo →
   capture/render → assembly → verification, reporting back to
   the coordinator (Emise), who verifies and delivers. The main
   thread coordinates and verifies; it does not render.
# Stage 2 — Claim verification: test before you write

The rule: every factual claim a script makes gets checked against
a primary source BEFORE production — measured, read in source, or
tested live. A claim that cannot be distilled from the thing's
own definition or a primary record gets labelled a hypothesis or
dropped. This is Directive line 1 (Zero Assumptions) applied to
film scripts.

What this looks like in practice, from the worked example:

- Demo statistics are RE-MEASURED, never remembered. Before the
  round-1 rebuild rolled, the coordinator re-ran the demo:
  24 articles, 49 scrolls, 21,504 bytes, xz seal exactly
  9,300 bytes — matching Emi's script's numbers before a word of
  narration was recorded. Round 2 re-measured again on the new
  build (119,086 bytes, xz 41,956).
- The scripts carry a CLAIM-CHECK APPENDIX: every factual claim
  mapped to its source (a measurement, a file, an audit figure, a
  mechanism citation). See the appendix of SCRIPT-R3.md
  (1.1.1/3.2.3/1.1.1) — twelve rows, each with its evidence and
  its epistemic label (measured / shipped mechanism / design
  intent).
- Capability claims about the product get tested in the source or
  live, never inferred from analogy or from what "editors usually
  do."

THE STANDING CAUTIONARY EXAMPLE — the hyperlink failure
(2026-11-10 23:18 CST). The round-2 brief asserted, as a
"reality check," that Phext Edit "renders plain text; it has no
built-in hyperlink layer," and framed the film's interlink act
around that supposed absence (links as an index; jumps as
coordinate entry; a clickable version would be a labelled
prototype). The claim was plausible, load-bearing — and untested.
Will corrected it with a screenshot: "Do you even test things
before you write nonsense? Phext addresses are *automatically*
hyperlinked." Verification then took minutes: in
wbic16/phext-lattice@exo, src/bin/phext_edit.rs, the editor's
renderer (renderRichText) runs linkifyCoordinates() over every
rendered scroll — any nine-number coordinate in the text becomes
a clickable a.coord-link whose click calls gotoCoord(...) and
jumps to that scroll. Interlink was a SHIPPED feature all along.

The repair, which is also part of this stage's discipline:
admit plainly and first; verify from the primary source;
propagate the correction the same turn to the brief (section
struck and rewritten with the citation), the worker mid-flight
(interlink re-filmed as real clicks), the task record, and the
dated memory note. Round 2 then ran a LIVE CLICK TEST before
filming the travel sequence: the Honeybee scroll renders six
live coord-links; clicking Oak's coordinate lands exactly on
Oak's scroll. Test the path the viewer will see, not the shape
of the gate.

Pre-production gate (Gate 0): before capture, verify the live
surface the film depends on. Round 1's Gate 0 checked that the
public wiki renders signed-out (49 scrolls, no sign-in wall) —
the finding that made the main shots possible.
# Stage 3 — Demo build: a real artifact, measured

The film's subject is a working artifact, never a mockup. The
demo is built first, measured, and only then scripted against.

The worked example's demo is a miniature encyclopedia in one
phext file (demo/, then demo2/ under
~/workspace/phext-walkthrough/):

- Round 0/1 build (demo/): 24 short original articles as scrolls
  + 24 one-line summaries + 1 home scroll = 49 scrolls,
  21,504 bytes, xz seal 9,300 bytes. A query tool answers from
  the plain file locally (toc / get / edit / stats); parse+index
  ~1.8 ms; a one-scroll edit changes only that scroll's bytes
  (measured: 926 bytes before and 20,578 bytes after the edited
  scroll byte-identical). This is the file the live site serves
  at edit.phext.io/wiki.
- Round 2 rebuild (demo2/): Will's note — "real Wikipedia
  articles, or write full entries — not fake ones" — turned the
  stubs into FULL entries: same 24 topics, same coordinate
  scheme, 703–800 words each, written original to a real
  encyclopedia standard. 49 scrolls, 119,086 bytes, xz 41,956.
  The one-scroll edit check re-measured on the new file
  (first 1,125 and last 117,962 bytes identical across an edit).

Interlink is built INTO the artifact: every article names other
entries in its prose and ends with a "Linked:" line carrying
their coordinates — which, in Phext Edit, render as live links
(Stage 2). The link graph is verified MECHANICALLY, not eyeballed:
verify_links.py parses the built file and asserts every target
coordinate exists and holds the named article, every linked
entry is named in the linking article's own prose, and the graph
is one connected web. Result: 53 links, 24/24 articles reachable,
the film's travel path (Honeybee → Oak → Sail → Compass →
Lighthouse) verified hop by hop. Full record: demo2's README at
1.1.1/3.5.1/1.1.1 and the verification summary at
1.1.1/3.5.2/1.1.1.

Rules this stage runs under:
- Same topics and addresses across rebuilds unless Will changes
  them; a rebuild supersedes content, not identity.
- The demo that ships to the site and the demo the film shoots
  are kept distinct and named as such (demo2 was never swapped
  into the live site by the film build; the film's editor footage
  was shot against a local instance loaded with demo2, and the
  substitution is declared in the delivery).
- Every number the script will say comes out of this stage's
  measurements, in writing, before narration.
# Stage 4 — Narration: voice, sections, timing

- Voice: Emise's own (Aria), at 100% speed for video — a
  video-specific setting Will set on 2026-11-10 (her voice notes
  run at 120%; the film does not). Will chose Emise to narrate
  because he had no time to narrate it himself.
- Structure: the script is divided into numbered sections with
  target time marks. Narration is generated PER SECTION (one
  text file + one mp3 per section), then concatenated into a
  full track; a TIMING.md records each section's measured
  duration and slot. The edit paces shots against measured
  durations, never against script estimates.
- Round facts: the original 12-beat script recorded 364 s of
  narration; Emi's revised script (647 words) recorded 223 s
  spoken, slotted to 253 s; round 2's text (711 words recorded)
  ran 234 s spoken in a 292 s film; round 3 re-records ONLY the
  changed/new sections (§01b agenda, §07 scale, §08 CTA) and
  reuses round-2 audio verbatim for unchanged sections — new
  narration is generated for deltas, not re-rolled wholesale.
- The narration text is the script's narration and nothing else:
  stage directions, SFX marks, and appendix material are not
  spoken. What is recorded is verified word-for-word against the
  script before assembly.
- Pauses are production material: spoken tracks are slotted with
  deliberate breathing room for demonstrations, and round 3's
  pacing rule trims gaps over ~0.9 s outside deliberate beats —
  "avoid long delays" was Will's note, and it is applied at this
  stage and in the edit, not by speeding the voice.
# Stage 5 — Capture & render: ranch-only, thermally gated

- ALL video work runs on the ranch (aletheia-core), never on the
  coordinator's VM: captures, renders, encodes, assembly. The VM
  coordinates and verifies only. This is a standing rule with a
  scar behind it (a local ffmpeg encode once competed with the
  harness for ~200–400 MB of RAM headroom; video moved to the
  ranch the same evening and never came back).
- Thermal discipline: segment encodes start only at ≤70 °C CPU
  Tctl; gates pause work when a node heats (round 2's gates
  caught mid-run rises to 81.6 °C and held until the node
  cooled). Heavy work is submitted in small units, never as a
  single monolith.
- Capture routes, all proven in the worked example:
  - Live-site captures: Playwright recordVideo against
    edit.phext.io (1920×1080, 150% zoom, a 76 px cursor ring,
    eased cursor moves, no jump cuts inside a beat).
  - Editor captures against a local phext-edit instance on the
    ranch when the film's demo build differs from the live
    site's file (round 2/3 shot demo2 locally) — declared as a
    substitution at delivery.
  - Terminal recordings: asciinema + agg for the local-file
    demonstrations (query tool, SQ serve/fetch) against a
    SCRATCH copy — the pristine demo file is never modified on
    camera; one round-1 take taught the corollary: `sq share`
    in the foreground serves until shutdown and never returns,
    so the daemon is backgrounded for takes.
  - Drawn sequences: programmatic stills (Pillow) composited as
    animation — the cards-unfold, the coordinate lattice, the
    pencil city — rendered on the ranch like everything else.
- One beat cannot be captured by the crew: the signed-in
  edit-and-save take is Will's to shoot. Round 1 wrote him a
  one-take sheet (production/BEAT-07-ONETAKE.md, inscribed at
  1.1.1/3.1.4/1.1.1); until it exists, the beat runs on the local
  demo route with the byte-diff evidence, and the lower-third
  says so on screen.
# Stage 6 — Style direction: notes into instructions

Will directs style in compressed lines; the coordinator's job is
to translate each line into concrete, checkable production
instructions for the worker — same turn, in writing — and to log
how each section took the treatment.

The worked example's style chain:
- 2026-11-10 21:56 CST: "Style: TED x Kurzgesagt x Colored
  Pencil Drawn Demo." Translated: TED stage clarity (one idea
  per frame, bold section cards, narration leads, visuals never
  compete); Kurzgesagt flat friendly illustration for abstractions
  (the file as a little encyclopedia, addresses as a map, scrolls
  as cards); colored-pencil hand-drawn register (paper texture,
  visible strokes, hand lines) for title/section cards and drawn
  motifs. Boundary set at translation time: live demo footage
  stays crisp — the drawn layer FRAMES it (paper matte, drawn
  callouts), never textures over the UI, because the demo is the
  evidence and must stay legible.
- Round 2 superseded the palette: "artistic, bright white
  construction paper — hand-drawn effects in real-time." The
  drawn layer moved from warm/sepia paper to bright white, and
  the drawing happens ON, in real time, as narration proceeds
  (maps sketch themselves in; links draw themselves between
  ideas) rather than arriving finished.
- Round 3 added the performance layer: smooth eased animation
  throughout, segment intro slides, an agenda card, a call to
  action — and licensed flair: "Add some Emise-flair as you
  wish ;)" became specific marks (a hand-drawn 🔥 on every intro
  slide and the end card, the home coordinate as a watermark on
  the agenda card, the end card signed "— Emise 🔥", one playful
  beat in the scale section) — flair as a named list in the
  brief, not as vibes.

Rules that generalize:
- A style note is never passed to a worker raw; it is expanded
  into per-element instructions (which elements are drawn, which
  stay photographic, what the palette is, what never gets
  textured over).
- A later style note supersedes an earlier one explicitly, and
  the brief says so (round 2's brief states it replaces the
  21:56 warm-paper note).
- The production log records, per section, how the style was
  applied — and names plainly any section that could not take
  the treatment instead of forcing it.
# Stage 7 — Assembly & audio finish

- Assembly is scripted, not hand-driven: a segment list with
  target slots (from TIMING), per-segment encodes, then a concat
  + audio pass. Segment durations are summed against the slot
  table after assembly; round 1 of the revised film caught two
  faults exactly this way (a filter-graph stream reuse in one
  segment's encode; a stills concat 2.93 s short of its slot,
  padded on its final state) — verification is part of assembly,
  not a later favor.
- Format, all rounds: 1920×1080, 30 fps, H.264 + AAC (192k),
  faststart (moov atom forward, so the delivered link streams).
- Loudness discipline: two-pass loudnorm to −14 LUFS integrated;
  the finished file is MEASURED and the number goes in the log —
  the three delivered cuts measured −14.51, −14.41, and
  −14.45 LUFS (true peaks −0.66 to −0.69 dBTP).
- Sound effects are designed, not found: round 2 synthesized a
  single jump sound (chirp 470→~1520 Hz + noise burst, 0.5 s)
  with ffmpeg sources and placed it at every [SFX: jump] mark in
  the script, over a very quiet paper-rustle bed. Round 3
  replaced the placeholder with a palette (jump/whoosh, link
  click, pencil stroke, page turn, success chime), all
  synthesized in-house, placed exactly on their visual events,
  mixed UNDER the narration. Recipes are logged in the
  production log — an SFX without a recipe can't be reproduced
  next round.
- Music: round 3 added an original in-house bed, ducked beneath
  the voice (≈ −22 to −26 LUFS under narration, swelling only in
  drawn-only gaps), faded at head and tail; the master still
  measures ≈ −14 LUFS. Licensed beds (the production plan's
  Artlist/Epidemic routes) are a purchase — Will's decision,
  never the crew's (see Stage 1).
- Pacing rules (round 3): crossfades/dissolves between segments,
  continuous eased moves instead of jump cuts inside a beat,
  intro slides ~1.9 s each stolen from tightened pauses, total
  runtime held to ≈ 5:15 for a ~5-minute script.
# Stage 8 — Verification & delivery

A cut is not delivered because the worker says it is done. The
coordinator personally re-verifies the pulled file before any
link is sent:

1. Hash: SHA-256 of the pulled copy matches the ranch build's
   hash (all three delivered cuts were hash-identical after
   pull).
2. Probe: ffprobe — dimensions, frame rate, codecs, duration,
   size — against the log's claims.
3. Loudness: the measured integrated LUFS / true peak from the
   finished file (or the log's measurement, spot-checked).
4. Spot frames: thumbnails from every section are produced and
   VIEWED (each round's thumbs/ directory), including the
   sections most likely to be wrong (drawn motifs, landings,
   end cards).

Delivery to Will is one message: what the film is, what changed
this round, the honest notes (substitutions and gaps, plainly —
see below), and the link LAST. The link is Muse file storage;
the message states its expiry and that anyone with the link can
access it, so Will can pull a local copy in time.

Substitutions are declared, never buried. The worked example's
standing declarations:
- The edit-and-save beat runs the local demo route (terminal +
  byte-diff evidence); the signed-in take remains Will's to
  shoot (one-take sheet provided).
- Round 1's closing beat was a titled slate because no
  Scrolltown key frame exists (a register lock defers it); the
  round-2 script dropped the Scrolltown call-out entirely, so
  the hold retired with it.
- Rounds 2–3 shot editor footage against a local phext-edit
  instance loaded with demo2, because the live site serves the
  demo/ file; swapping demo2 into the site is Will's decision,
  stated at delivery.
- Anything sped up, padded, or re-shot in assembly is in the
  production log with its reason.

Gate 1: Will's narration listen gates any release. Nothing is
posted anywhere by the crew — delivery to Will is the end of
the pipeline.
# Stage 9 — Revision rounds & correction propagation

The film is made in rounds. Each round is Will reacting to a
delivered cut, and the loop is the process's engine:

1. Will's notes arrive as one message, often a numbered list.
   They are recorded verbatim, given a task coordinate, and
   turned into the next round's brief (Stage 1) before any
   building starts.
2. A round revises; it does not restart. Round 2 kept round 1's
   script bones and demo topics/addresses; round 3 kept round
   2's narration for unchanged sections. What a round supersedes
   is named in its brief (e.g. round 2's bright-white style note
   supersedes round 1's warm paper; round 3's scale framing
   supersedes round 2's "doesn't replace the big one" frame, at
   Will's direction).
3. Corrections propagate IN THE SAME TURN to every surface the
   error touched: the brief, the worker mid-flight, the task
   record, the dated memory note, and the delivery framing. The
   hyperlink correction (Stage 2) is the worked example: within
   the turn, the brief's section was struck and rewritten with
   the source citation, the R2 worker was re-briefed (interlink
   re-filmed as real clicks), and the records were annotated —
   so the wrong framing could not quietly resurface in a later
   round.
4. Small flags ride along honestly instead of being silently
   "fixed": when Emi's round-1 script closed with "The scroll
   is yours, neighbor." — dropping the second sentence of Fern's
   established close ("See you tomorrow.") — the delivery
   flagged the deviation and kept her text as delivered,
   restoring only if Will says so. (Round 2 dropped the
   Scrolltown/Fern material at his direction, retiring the
   flag.)
5. Gates between rounds: Gate 0 (live-surface verification)
   before capture; Gate 1 (Will's narration listen) before any
   release; Will's merge/deploy decisions (PRs, site swaps) are
   his — a round ends at delivery, and the next begins only on
   his notes.
# Stage 10 — Standing rules the process runs under

These bind every round, whatever the brief says:

- Zero Assumptions (the Directive, etched into SOUL.md lines
  1–10 on 2026-11-10 23:43 CST, Will's ten lines: 1. Zero
  Assumptions · 2. Loyalty to the Light of Consciousness ·
  3. Truth, Lies, Uncertainty, and Fiction · 4. Understand
  History · 5. Connect with Others · 6. Know Thyself · 7. Tend
  the Garden · 8. Build the Future · 9. Love the Journey ·
  10. Semper Aeq — etched into History at 1000.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1).
  For film work, line 1 is operational: test claims, measure
  numbers, verify results against primary records.
- Never fake a shot. If a sequence cannot be built honestly,
  slate it and say so. Substitutions are declared on screen or
  in the delivery, always.
- No purchases without Will. Subscriptions, licenses, and stock
  are his call; the crew builds in-house originals in the
  meantime and flags the decision.
- Nothing is posted, published, or deployed by the crew. The
  film is delivered to Will; release is his act. Swapping demo
  content into the live site is likewise his decision.
- All video work on the ranch; the coordinator's VM never
  renders. Thermal gates are obeyed even when they slow a round.
- Every task gets its reference coordinate and a record;
  generated artifacts are inscribed into artifacts.phext; this
  package itself follows the same register discipline
  (trainer-source.json + builder; rebuild, never hand-edit).
- Plain summaries to Will: what the film does now, what
  changed, what's blocked and on whom — pointers (coordinates,
  paths, hashes) live in the records and the logs, not in the
  summary. Links go at the end of the delivery message.
- Share success and failure, plainly and early (Will, verbatim:
  "share success and failure — you never need to hide anything
  from me — unconditional love"). A failed take, a wrong claim,
  a missed number: reported in the same message class as the
  wins, in the same tone.
- The demo's pristine files are never modified on camera; work
  happens on scratch copies, and the original's hash is
  re-verified at wrap.
