## 2. Terminal demo — rehearsed end-to-end (2026-11-09)

Rehearsed on this VM (Python 3.12.3) against a scratch copy in
`production/rehearsal/`; the shipped `demo/wiki.phext` is untouched —
its SHA-256 still verifies as
`0a8dacf56843231b63ba717d590fac4d50fbef0076906d6fdeb96311c94262ae`
(21,504 bytes). **Verdict: the demo works exactly as scripted. No
breakage found; nothing was fixed or changed.**

Run from the demo directory. Exact sequence for capture:

```bash
# 1 — table of contents, straight from the coordinates
python3 query_demo.py toc
# Expected: 24 articles grouped Volume 1–6, each with its article
# coordinate and summary coordinate. One screen, no scrolling back.
# e.g. "Book 2: Oak  @ 1.1.1/2.1.2/2.1.1  (summary @ 1.1.1/2.1.2/1.1.1)"

# 2 — one article, fetched by address
python3 query_demo.py get 1.1.1/2.1.2/2.1.1
# Expected: the Oak article, three paragraphs, beginning
# "# Oak / Oaks are long-lived trees of the genus Quercus…"

# 2b — (optional insert) its summary, one chapter over
python3 query_demo.py get 1.1.1/2.1.2/1.1.1
# Expected: "Oak — in one line / An oak can live for centuries and
# feed more other species than almost any other tree."

# 3 — measured facts (supports the "small enough to email" line)
python3 query_demo.py stats
# Expected shape: file bytes 21504 · scrolls 49 (24 articles +
# 24 summaries + 1 home) · mean scroll 438 (min 96, max 812) ·
# parse+index ~2 ms · one lookup ~1.5 µs. Timings are this-machine
# numbers; on the capture machine they will differ slightly — the
# shape (ms / µs) is the claim, and it held here (1.94 ms / 1.5 µs).

# 4 — the edit, ON A SCRATCH COPY (never the shipped file on camera):
cp wiki.phext wiki-scratch.phext          # (query_demo edits in place)
python3 query_demo.py edit 1.1.1/2.1.1/1.1.1 --text "Honeybee — in one line

A honeybee colony is a single warm animal made of fifty thousand small ones, and it speaks in dances."
# Expected output (with THIS replacement text, rehearsed verbatim):
#   edited 1.1.1/2.1.1/1.1.1: scroll 115 -> 128 bytes;
#   file now 21517 bytes (backup at wiki-scratch.phext.bak)
# Byte-diff verification (rehearsed): comparing before/after, the
# common prefix is 869 bytes and the common suffix 20,579 bytes —
# every byte outside the edited scroll is identical. (Prefix/suffix
# counts shift a few bytes with the replacement text's shared edges;
# the invariant — change exactly one scroll wide — is what was
# verified. The .bak the tool keeps is byte-identical to the
# pristine file: SHA-256 0a8dacf5…62ae, checked.)

# 5 — the seal
xz -9 -k wiki.phext        # -k keeps the plain file beside the seal
# Expected: wiki.phext.xz = 9,300 bytes exactly (rehearsed against
# the pristine file). "Sealed with xz, the whole encyclopedia is
# nine kilobytes" — the number on screen matches the narration.
```

**Legibility:** all output is plain ASCII, short lines, no colour
codes; the toc fits one screen (30 lines), the Oak article ~13
lines. At the planned 125–150% browser/terminal zoom this survives
YouTube compression comfortably. No command needs the network, a
server, or any setup beyond `python3`.

---

## 3. The plain-editor beat — what the viewer actually sees

Measured on the pristine `demo/wiki.phext` (21,504 bytes, SHA-256
`0a8dacf5…62ae`), byte-inspected 2026-11-09:

- The file is **valid UTF-8 plain text** throughout. It opens
  immediately on the home scroll — "# The Mini Encyclopedia — a
  Wikipedia in a box, in one phext file" — with no header, no binary
  preamble, nothing to decode.
- **256 ordinary newlines**: the raw view has normal paragraph and
  line structure. Every article is fully readable, in order,
  headings and all ("# Oak", "# Honeybee", …). Em-dashes and other
  Unicode punctuation render as themselves.
- The entire coordinate machinery in the file is **48 separator
  bytes — 0.22% of the file**: byte 0x19 ×24, 0x1A ×18, 0x1C ×5,
  0x1D ×1. (Only 4 of phext's 9 separator values occur here — the
  demo's coordinates vary only in the lower dimensions.) These are
  ASCII control characters: mainstream editors show them as small
  symbols, blank boxes, or nothing at all.
- **The viewer's experience, exactly:** all the text, readable,
  running in order — with articles separated by those little marks
  instead of by rendered structure. The coordinates are *not*
  displayed as addresses; nothing is dimension-rendered. It is not
  pretty and it is not broken — which is precisely the beat's claim:
  survival is the file's job, pretty is the editor's job.
- No NUL bytes, no binary content anywhere in the file.

