# Beat 07 — Edit one scroll, save · ONE-TAKE SHEET

**Slot in the cut:** 25.5 s (narration `narration/beat-07.mp3`). The
rough cut holds a titled placeholder slate of exactly this length;
this sheet produces the real take that replaces it in the final.
**Shoot for ~35 s of raw take** so the edit can trim head and tail.

**Surface:** Phext Edit, signed in as Will, on the hosted example
wiki — `https://edit.phext.io/wiki/#1.1.1/2.1.1/1.1.1`
(bottom bar must read **📂 wiki.phext**).

**The scroll:** the Honeybee one-line summary,
**1.1.1/2.1.1/1.1.1** (Volume 2 "Earth and Sky" area, Book 1,
Chapter 1). Same scroll the terminal beat (09) edits on a scratch
copy — the two beats deliberately mirror each other.

**The change — exactly one line.** The title line stays untouched.
The body line changes from:

> A honeybee colony is a single organism made of tens of thousands of cooperating insects.

to:

> A honeybee colony is a single warm animal made of fifty thousand small ones, and it speaks in dances.

**⚠ This save is real.** It writes to the hosted, public example
wiki and persists. The original line is recorded verbatim above;
restoring it is the same one-edit pass with the texts swapped.

---

## Pre-flight (before rolling)

1. Sign in at edit.phext.io (Google / GitHub / X). Confirm the
   top banner is gone / the bar shows your session, and the file
   picker shows **wiki.phext**.
2. Open `https://edit.phext.io/wiki/#1.1.1/2.1.1/1.1.1`.
   Confirm the header reads **💎 1.1.1/2.1.1/1.1.1 ◆ Scroll**,
   **[49 scrolls]**, and the pane shows "Honeybee — in one line"
   with the ORIGINAL body line above.
3. Capture settings, same as the rest of the shoot: big video
   cursor on, text bumped up (browser zoom 150%).
4. Put the replacement body line on the clipboard (copy it from
   this sheet). You will paste, not type — one clean pass.

## The take (roll, then in order)

| t≈ | Action |
|---|---|
| 0 s | Start on the Honeybee summary, cursor resting mid-pane. Hold 2 s. |
| 2 s | Glide the cursor to **✎ Edit** in the bottom bar and click it (or press `e`). The scroll opens in the editor. |
| 5 s | Glide into the text. Select the whole body line (triple-click it, or click at its start then Shift+click at its end). The title line must NOT be selected. |
| 9 s | Paste (Ctrl+V). The body line is now the new text. Nothing else in the editor changed. |
| 12 s | Pause 2 s with the cursor resting beside the changed line — let the viewer read it. |
| 14 s | Glide to the editor toolbar's **Save (Ctrl+S)** button and click it (or press Ctrl+S). |
| 16 s | You're back on the scroll view. **Hold 3+ s**: the summary now reads with the new line; header still 💎 1.1.1/2.1.1/1.1.1; [49 scrolls] unchanged. |
| 20 s+ | Keep holding to ~30–35 s raw, then stop. The edit trims to the 25.5 s slot. |

## Expected on-screen result (the verification glance)

- Same coordinate in the header before and after: **1.1.1/2.1.1/1.1.1**.
- The scroll's body is the new line; the title line is untouched.
- Scroll count still **[49 scrolls]**; no other scroll was opened
  or changed during the take.
- That stillness *is* the beat's claim: one scroll changed, the
  other 48 byte-identical — the same fact beat 09 proves on the
  local file (115 → 128 bytes, prefix/suffix verified).

## If it goes wrong

- **Save button not where expected:** the control is a green
  **Save (Ctrl+S)** button in the editor toolbar (verified in the
  phext-lattice source, `saveScroll()`). Ctrl+S alone also saves.
- **Wrong line selected / title line touched:** stop the take,
  close the editor WITHOUT saving, re-check pre-flight step 2,
  roll again. Do not improvise a fix on camera.
- **Anything else unexpected:** stop, note what the screen showed,
  and hand it back — the placeholder slate stays in the cut until
  a clean take exists. Nothing about this beat gets faked.
