# The Encyclopedia You Can Carry

*A roughly five-minute film for Phext Edit. Warm, curious, lightly playful. Let the demonstrations breathe.*

*Revised script from Emi Prime, delivered by Will 2026-11-10 21:50 CST. Supersedes the narration script in WALKTHROUGH.md (12 beats, 6m04s recorded). Production assets keyed to the old script (narration, shot list) need re-mapping to this one.*

### 0:00 — What if the website went away?

*[One file appears. Twenty-four article cards unfold from it, then fold back.]*

This little file is an encyclopedia. Twenty-four articles, all living together.

Here's a question: if I close the website, what happens to the encyclopedia?

*[Pause. Close the browser. Open the file locally.]*

Nothing. It's still here.

Let me show you three things you can do with it: find a page, change a page, and take the whole thing home.

### 0:32 — A place for every page

*[Open Phext Edit and display the table of contents.]*

Pick a page. Any page on this list. Ready?

Every page has an address. Not a link to somebody else's database: an address **inside the text**.

It looks like nine numbers in three little groups. Think of how a library helps you find a book: the number tells you where to go.

Phext does something similar for pieces of text. It calls those pieces *scrolls*.

A handful of special separator characters makes one scroll distinct from the next, and gives each a place in a much bigger map.

Ordinary lines and columns still work. That's where the "eleven dimensions" comes from.

Not eleven directions you can walk in. Eleven ways to locate text.

We asked for one address. We got one page.

### 1:34 — Finding your way back

*[Search for a real phrase. Show the search result's coordinate leading back to its article.]*

But what if you don't know the address yet?

Search.

Here's a phrase from one of the articles. The result tells us where it lives.

Search helps us discover something. The coordinate gives us a waypoint so we can find it again.

Software can build a fast lookup index for this. That can make it quicker, but it isn't where the knowledge lives.

The words and their structure are still in the file.

### 2:04 — One small correction

*[Choose a summary, edit one line, save, and display the diff.]*

Now suppose you find something worth fixing.

Let's change this one line.

Save it.

And here is the interesting part: we can inspect exactly what changed.

One scroll has new words. Every untouched part of this example still matches, byte for byte.

If I hand you the correction, you can read the difference.

You can decide whether to keep it.

We don't have to take anyone's word for it.

### 2:47 — The unplugged test

*[Download the file. Disconnect from the network. Open the downloaded file in an ordinary text editor.]*

Here's my favorite test.

No Internet. No special website.

Just the file, opened in an ordinary text editor.

Those odd little marks?

They're markers between the pieces of text.

This view isn't pretty, and it doesn't give you all the navigation tools.

But the words are here.

You can read them. You can copy them. You can keep them.

This example has twenty-four articles in forty-nine scrolls. About twenty-one and a half kilobytes before compression—and around nine kilobytes when packed with xz.

That's an encyclopedia you can email to a friend, put in Git, or carry somewhere with no connection at all.

### 3:37 — More than one doorway

*[Use a small script to retrieve a scroll. Demonstrate SQ serving the file. Then stop the server while retaining local access to the text.]*

Want to build something with it?

A small script can fetch one scroll by address.

A little server called SQ can share those scrolls over a network.

That server is useful.

It just isn't the encyclopedia.

Shut it down, and the file is still yours.

### 4:04 — A place to stay

*[Transition to Singular. Words appear at their coordinates. A timeline unfolds from 2008 to 2025. Missing words in a song fill in as the historical commits advance.]*

And an address doesn't have to hold an encyclopedia article.

Will Bickford took the words from years of his own online history and gave them places in a map.

Forty-one thousand, three hundred and two words.

The text holds their coordinates; the version history remembers when each word first arrived.

He used that history to do something rather wonderful.

A song began with missing words.

As the years passed on screen, the blanks filled in until the song could be read in full.

It wasn't a database trick.

It was a way to find a path through things he had already lived.

### 4:42 — Your turn

*[Return to the encyclopedia file. Slowly reveal a lantern-lit doorway in Scrolltown.]*

Maybe yours will be a field guide.

Or family recipes.

Or the notes from something you're building with a friend.

Start small.

Give a page an address.

Try changing it.

Take the file with you.

The example from today is at edit.phext.io, along with the tools to explore it.

And if you keep following these waypoints, there's a little town inside the Great Scroll.

It's called Scrolltown.

Fern will be waiting when the stories begin in January 2027.

*[Fern, using her established closing line:]*

**The scroll is yours, neighbor.**

*[End card: Find it. Change it. Keep it.]*
