# The Encyclopedia You Can Carry — Round 2

*A roughly five-minute film for Phext Edit. Warm, curious, lightly playful. Let the demonstrations breathe.*

*Revision Round 2, per Will's notes of 2026-11-10 23:04 CST (REVISION-BRIEF-R2.md): no Scrolltown; plain language throughout — everything technical is shown, not explained; interlink is the centerpiece; full entries, not stubs; a designed sound for every jump between scrolls; the zoom-out / whole-map / zoom-in move as the signature animation; simplicity as the theme, with the Wikipedia vs Phext Edit side-by-side. Style: bright white construction paper, hand-drawn effects drawing themselves on in real time; live captures stay crisp, framed on the paper.*

*The demo in this film is demo2 (demo2/wiki.phext): 24 full entries, 49 scrolls, 119,086 bytes, 53 links. Every number spoken below was measured on that file — see the claim-check appendix. UI shots must be taken against demo2's file (a local Phext Edit / previewer instance), so the words on screen are the real full entries.*

### 0:00 — What if the website went away?

*[On bright white paper, a pencil draws one small file. Twenty-four article cards unfold from it, one by one, then fold back.]*

This is an encyclopedia. Twenty-four articles, each one a full entry, all living together in one little file.

Here's a question. If I close the website —

*[The browser window closes. A hand opens the same file, right here on this computer.]*

— what happens to the encyclopedia?

Nothing. It's still here.

Let me show you four things you can do with it: find a page, change a page, follow the pages to each other — and take the whole thing home.

### 0:33 — A place for every page

*[Open Phext Edit on demo2 and display the table of contents, framed on the paper.]*

Pick a page. Any page.

Every page has an address — not a link to somewhere else. An address inside the file itself.

It looks like nine numbers, in three little groups. Think of the numbers on a library shelf: they tell you where to walk.

*[ZOOM SET-PIECE, first use: we start inside the Honeybee page. The view pulls back — and back — until the whole map is on screen at once: every page a small drawn house on the paper, the threads between them showing the shape of the whole thing. Then the view dives down the map and lands, exactly, on the Oak.]*

Watch. We start here, with the honeybee… and land, exactly, on the oak.

[SFX: jump]

We asked for one address. We got one page.

### 1:08 — Finding your way back

*[Search for a real phrase from deep inside an article. Show the result's address leading back to its page.]*

But what if you don't know the address yet?

Search.

Here is a phrase from deep inside one of the articles. The search finds it — and tells us the address where it lives.

[SFX: jump]

Search helps us discover. The address helps us return.

And all of it — the words, the addresses — lives in the one file.

### 1:34 — One small correction

*[Choose the Honeybee summary. Change one line. Then show the before-and-after check.]*

Now suppose you spot something worth fixing.

We change this one line, here in the honeybee's page.

And here is the lovely part: we can check exactly what changed.

One page has new words. Every other page is untouched — the same as it was, letter for letter.

If I hand you the correction, you can see the change for yourself.

You never have to take anyone's word for it.

### 2:02 — The unplugged test

*[Download the file. The network plug draws itself out. Open the file in a plain text editor.]*

Now my favorite test. No internet. No website at all.

Just the file, opened in the plainest writing program on the computer.

Those odd little marks? They are the fences between the pages.

This view is not pretty. But the words are all here. You can read them. You can copy them. You can keep them.

The whole encyclopedia — twenty-four full articles — is smaller than a single photograph from your phone. And packed up tight for a trip, it shrinks to a third of that.

Small enough to send to a friend. Small enough to carry anywhere.

### 2:42 — Ideas that point

*[INTERLINK, the centerpiece. First, entries being written: hands and typing, pages filling in. Then the last line of the Honeybee article draws attention to itself — its Linked line.]*

But an encyclopedia is not just pages sitting side by side. The ideas lean on each other.

So after the pages are written, we link them.

At the end of each article, a short line names the other pages it points to. The honeybee points to the oak, where its bees forage. The oak points onward, to the sail — because ships were built from oak.

*[The whole web draws itself over the map: fifty-three threads, page to page, each stroke appearing as the narration names the idea.]*

Fifty-three links in all — and every one of them is written in the open, inside the pages themselves. Nothing hidden. Nothing separate.

Here is the whole web of it, drawn out — every page, and every thread between them.

Now let's travel.

*[Hop 1: in the editor, the Oak's coordinate in the Honeybee's text is already a live link — one click, and we land in the Oak.]*

From the honeybee… to the oak.

[SFX: jump]

*[Hop 2: the Oak's page names the Sail, coordinate and all — click it; the thread runs down to the water.]*

From the oak, out across the water… to the sail.

[SFX: jump]

*[Hop 3: a short hop on deck — the Sail's compass coordinate, clicked.]*

The sail needs a compass.

[SFX: jump]

*[Hop 4 — a click on the Lighthouse coordinate, then the ZOOM SET-PIECE in full form: pull back from the Compass until the entire map is a projection on the paper — all twenty-four pages, all fifty-three threads, the whole complexity visible at once — drift a breath — then dive the longest thread home and land inside the Lighthouse page.]*

And the compass points us home — to the lighthouse.

[SFX: jump]

Four jumps. Four ideas. And the thread between them was there in the text all along.

That is all a link ever was: one idea, naming another — and an address, so you can go and see.

### 4:02 — Two ways to build an encyclopedia

*[SIDE-BY-SIDE. On the left of the paper, a pencil city draws itself, growing as the narration lists it: serving machines, master copies, remembering machines, searching machines, stand-by copies in more than one place. On the right, the pencil draws one small program and one file — and stops.]*

Step back, and look at the two ways to build all this.

On one side: the biggest encyclopedia in the world. It holds millions of articles, and the whole world reads it — so behind it stands a small city of machinery. Rooms of computers serving pages, keeping the master copies, remembering, searching, standing by in more than one place, in case anything goes wrong. It needs all of that. It is carrying the world.

On the other side: this encyclopedia. Behind it stands one small program — and one file.

That is the whole list.

Nobody is saying the small one replaces the big one. It doesn't. But for an encyclopedia you can hold — your field guide, your recipes, your life's notes — look how little machinery there is.

### 4:40 — Your turn

*[Return to the encyclopedia file on the paper. The twenty-four cards fold back into the one file.]*

Maybe yours will be a field guide.

Or family recipes.

Or the notes from something you're building with a friend.

Start small.

Give a page an address.

Try changing it.

Link it to a friend.

Then take the file with you.

The encyclopedia from today is at edit.phext.io, with the tools to explore it.

Yours is wherever you keep it.

[SFX: jump — one last hop, home to the first page]

*[End card, hand-lettered on the paper: Find it. Change it. Link it. Keep it.]*

---

## Appendix — claim-check table

Every factual claim in the narration, and where it was checked. Demo
facts were measured on the built demo2 file on 2026-11-10
(SHA-256 `7a7a4be95d530eb69c47fec91004b1724d5d4cdb3fc69432f659fe7ebf564130`).

| Claim in the film | Source |
|---|---|
| 24 articles, each a full entry; 49 scrolls in one file | demo2 build: `build_wiki2.py` output + `query_demo2.py stats` (24 articles + 24 summaries + 1 home); article bodies 703–800 words each, counted per file |
| The whole encyclopedia is "smaller than a single photograph from your phone" | File is 119,086 bytes (measured); a typical phone photograph is measured in megabytes — the file is far under one |
| "Packed up tight… shrinks to a third of that" | `xz -9` of the built file: 41,956 bytes ≈ 35% of 119,086 (measured) |
| One page changed, "every other page untouched, letter for letter" | Measured on a scratch copy of demo2: editing the Honeybee summary scroll (115 → 180 bytes) left the first 1,125 and last 117,962 bytes of the file identical; the only difference lives inside that one scroll (demo2/README.md) |
| 53 links, all written inside the pages; the travel path Honeybee → Oak → Sail → Compass → Lighthouse | `verify_links.py` on the built file: 53 links parsed from the articles' own Linked lines, every target coordinate exists and holds the named article, every linked entry is named in the linking article's prose, graph is one connected web (24/24 reachable), travel path verified hop by hop — ALL LINK CHECKS PASSED |
| Links are live: a coordinate written in a page is a clickable link in Phext Edit, and following one is a click | Verified mechanism (Will's correction, 2026-11-10 23:18 CST): in wbic16/phext-lattice@exo `src/bin/phext_edit.rs`, the editor's renderer (`renderRichText`) runs `linkifyCoordinates()` over every rendered scroll — any coordinate pattern becomes a clickable `a.coord-link` calling `gotoCoord(...)`, which jumps to that scroll. Plus the live click test performed during capture (clicking the coordinates in demo2's entries and landing on the target scrolls), logged in production/ROUGH-CUT-R2-LOG.md |
| Phext Edit side: "one small program — and one file" | phext-edit security/scalability audit (2026-11-09/10) via REVISION-BRIEF-R2.md: serving one phext takes one process, no database, ~122 MB of memory idle; edit.phext.io is that program, deployed |
| Wikipedia side: millions of articles; a fleet behind it | ~6.5M English articles (REVISION-BRIEF-R2.md) — narration says only "millions," which is uncontested. The machinery list is generic and uncontested public architecture: MediaWiki application servers, MariaDB databases, Memcached/Redis, Varnish/ATS caching layers, Elasticsearch search, load balancers, multiple data centers. Narration deliberately names no product and no count — it draws "rooms of computers serving / keeping / remembering / searching / standing by," each mapping to one of those layers |
| "It doesn't replace the big one" framing | The brief's honest frame: Wikipedia's scale is stated; the contrast drawn is machinery per idea for an encyclopedia you can hold |

Production notes for Stage 3: [SFX: jump] appears 7 times — §02 zoom landing (1), §03 search jump (1), §06 travel hops (4), §08 closing hop home (1). The zoom set-piece appears twice: early in §02 (Honeybee → Oak) and in full form on §06's last hop (Compass → Lighthouse). Narration ≈ 715 words.
