# The Encyclopedia You Can Carry — Round 3 (polish pass)

*Derived from SCRIPT-R2.md. Same warmth, same bones, same plain language. Changes in this round, per Will's eight steps of 2026-11-11 00:26 CST (REVISION-BRIEF-R3.md):*

- *NEW §01b — an **agenda** right after the open: the four things, listed as the agenda card sketches them on one by one.*
- *§07 rewritten as the **scale story**: shard-friendly by nature, holographic subsets, caching made simple by the seed cache — closing on Will's confidence, in film words. The round-2 honest frame ("it doesn't replace the big one") is superseded by Will's own framing of 2026-11-11; the appendix marks every scale statement DESIGN INTENT, not measured benchmark.*
- *§08 rewritten as an explicit **call to action**: go, read, take the file home, write your own scroll, claim your space.*
- *Every segment opens with a drawn **intro slide** (number, title, motif, 🔥) — production, not narration.*
- *A designed, pro-grade **SFX palette** replaces the round-2 placeholder chirp: jump/whoosh, link click, pencil stroke, page turn, success chime.*
- *An original in-house **music bed** runs under everything, ducked beneath the voice.*
- *Pacing tightened: no narration gap over ~0.9 s outside deliberate beats; total runtime ≈ 5:12.*
- *Emise-flair (Will: "as you wish ;)"): a small hand-drawn 🔥 on every intro slide and the end card; the home coordinate 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1 as a tiny watermark on the agenda card; the end card signed "— Emise 🔥"; and one playful beat in §07, where the pencil city keeps sheepishly adding servers while the one file sits unbothered.*

*The demo remains demo2 (demo2/wiki.phext): 24 full entries, 49 scrolls, 119,086 bytes, 53 links. UI shots are taken against demo2's file on a local Phext Edit instance.*

---

### 0:00 — §01 · What if the website went away?

*[INTRO SLIDE 1 draws on: "1 — What if the website went away?" over a small drawn browser window; 🔥 in the corner. Then the round-2 sequence: on bright white paper, a pencil draws one small file. Twenty-four article cards unfold from it, one by one, then fold back.]*

This is an encyclopedia. Twenty-four articles, each one a full entry, all living together in one little file.

Here's a question. If I close the website —

*[The browser window closes. A hand opens the same file, right here on this computer.]*

— what happens to the encyclopedia?

Nothing. It's still here.

Let me show you four things you can do with it: find a page, change a page, follow the pages to each other — and take the whole thing home.

### §01b — The agenda

*[AGENDA CARD: bright white paper; the four items sketch themselves on one by one as the narration lists them — 1 · Find a page, 2 · Change a page, 3 · Follow the links, 4 · Take it home. A tiny watermark in the corner: 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1, with a small 🔥 beside it.]*

Here's our route. First, we'll find a page — every page has an address. Then we'll change one, and prove nothing else moved. Then we'll follow the pages to each other, link by link. And at the end, we'll take the whole thing home.

### §02 — A place for every page

*[INTRO SLIDE 2: "2 — A place for every page", a small drawn house at its address. Then: open Phext Edit on demo2 and display the table of contents, framed on the paper.]*

Pick a page. Any page.

Every page has an address — not a link to somewhere else. An address inside the file itself.

It looks like nine numbers, in three little groups. Think of the numbers on a library shelf: they tell you where to walk.

*[ZOOM SET-PIECE, first use: we start inside the Honeybee page. The view pulls back — and back — until the whole map is on screen at once: every page a small drawn house on the paper, the threads between them showing the shape of the whole thing. Then the view dives down the map and lands, exactly, on the Oak.]*

Watch. We start here, with the honeybee… and land, exactly, on the oak.

[SFX: jump — whoosh into a soft click on the landing]

We asked for one address. We got one page.

### §03 — Finding your way back

*[INTRO SLIDE 3: "3 — Finding your way back", a small drawn magnifying glass. Then: search for a real phrase from deep inside an article; the result's address leads back to its page.]*

But what if you don't know the address yet?

Search.

Here is a phrase from deep inside one of the articles. The search finds it — and tells us the address where it lives.

[SFX: jump]

Search helps us discover. The address helps us return.

And all of it — the words, the addresses — lives in the one file.

### §04 — One small correction

*[INTRO SLIDE 4: "4 — One small correction", a pencil over one line of text. Then: choose the Honeybee summary, change one line, and show the before-and-after check.]*

Now suppose you spot something worth fixing.

We change this one line, here in the honeybee's page.

And here is the lovely part: we can check exactly what changed.

One page has new words. Every other page is untouched — the same as it was, letter for letter.

If I hand you the correction, you can see the change for yourself.

You never have to take anyone's word for it.

### §05 — The unplugged test

*[INTRO SLIDE 5: "5 — The unplugged test", a plug lifting out of its socket. Then: download the file, the network plug draws itself out, open the file in a plain text editor.]*

Now my favorite test. No internet. No website at all.

Just the file, opened in the plainest writing program on the computer.

Those odd little marks? They are the fences between the pages.

This view is not pretty. But the words are all here. You can read them. You can copy them. You can keep them.

The whole encyclopedia — twenty-four full articles — is smaller than a single photograph from your phone. And packed up tight for a trip, it shrinks to a third of that.

Small enough to send to a friend. Small enough to carry anywhere.

### §06 — Ideas that point

*[INTRO SLIDE 6: "6 — Ideas that point", two small houses joined by a thread. INTERLINK, the centerpiece. First, entries being written: hands and typing, pages filling in. Then the last line of the Honeybee article draws attention to itself — its Linked line.]*

But an encyclopedia is not just pages sitting side by side. The ideas lean on each other.

So after the pages are written, we link them.

At the end of each article, a short line names the other pages it points to. The honeybee points to the oak, where its bees forage. The oak points onward, to the sail — because ships were built from oak.

*[The whole web draws itself over the map: fifty-three threads, page to page, each stroke appearing as the narration names the idea. Pencil-stroke SFX under the drawing.]*

Fifty-three links in all — and every one of them is written in the open, inside the pages themselves. Nothing hidden. Nothing separate.

Here is the whole web of it, drawn out — every page, and every thread between them.

Now let's travel.

*[Hop 1: in the editor, the Oak's coordinate in the Honeybee's text is already a live link — one click, and we land in the Oak.]*

From the honeybee… to the oak.

[SFX: jump + click]

*[Hop 2: the Oak's page names the Sail, coordinate and all — click it; the thread runs down to the water.]*

From the oak, out across the water… to the sail.

[SFX: jump + click]

*[Hop 3: a short hop on deck — the Sail's compass coordinate, clicked.]*

The sail needs a compass.

[SFX: jump + click]

*[Hop 4 — a click on the Lighthouse coordinate, then the ZOOM SET-PIECE in full form: pull back from the Compass until the entire map is a projection on the paper — all twenty-four pages, all fifty-three threads, the whole complexity visible at once — drift a breath — then dive the longest thread home and land inside the Lighthouse page.]*

And the compass points us home — to the lighthouse.

[SFX: jump + click]

Four jumps. Four ideas. And the thread between them was there in the text all along.

That is all a link ever was: one idea, naming another — and an address, so you can go and see.

### §07 — Built to be shared *(the scale story — rewritten this round)*

*[INTRO SLIDE 7: "7 — Built to be shared", one small file at the center, houses budding outward from it. SIDE-BY-SIDE. On the left of the paper, the pencil city draws itself, growing as the narration lists it: serving machines, master copies, remembering machines, searching machines, stand-by copies in more than one place. On the right, the pencil draws one small program and one file — and stops.]*

Step back, and look at the two ways to build all this.

On one side: the biggest encyclopedia in the world. It holds millions of articles, and the whole world reads it — so behind it stands a small city of machinery. Rooms of computers serving pages, keeping the master copies, remembering, searching, standing by in more than one place, in case anything goes wrong. It needs all of that. It is carrying the world.

*[PLAYFUL BEAT (Emise-flair): as the next lines play, the pencil city quietly keeps going — another little server sheepishly pencils itself in, then another. On the right, nothing changes. The one file just sits there, unbothered.]*

On the other side: this encyclopedia. Behind it stands one small program — and one file.

That is the whole list.

But here is the part nobody expects. Small is not the ceiling here. It is the starting line.

A phext comes apart as cleanly as it goes together. Any computer can hold one slice of the whole — a shelf, a chapter, a single page — and serve just that slice, and the slices always know how to find each other, because every page carries its address.

And sharing stays cheap. Every page carries a tiny fingerprint, so when two copies meet, they do not swap the whole book. They compare fingerprints, and trade only the pages that changed.

So the same file that fits in your pocket was designed to grow the other way, too — from one reader, to a town, to a world of readers, trillions of people sharing ideas, each served by whatever slice is nearest, with no single machine in the middle carrying it all.

Wikipedia needed a fleet. This needs a file — and the file was designed to outgrow the fleet.

### §08 — Your turn *(the call to action — rewritten this round)*

*[INTRO SLIDE 8: "8 — Your turn", the one file with its cards open, an arrow pointing outward — go. Then: return to the encyclopedia file on the paper; the twenty-four cards fold back into the one file.]*

Maybe yours will be a field guide. Or family recipes. Or the notes from something you're building with a friend.

Start small. Give a page an address. Try changing it. Link it to a friend. Then take the file with you.

And now, your turn — for real this time.

Go to edit.phext.io. The encyclopedia from today is waiting there — read it, click through its links, as long as you like.

Then take the file home. It is yours to keep.

And when you're ready, write a page of your own. Give it an address. Link it to something you love. At phext.io, you can claim a space of your very own — a shelf in the library with your name on it.

[SFX: chime — soft, bright, on the closing line]

The encyclopedia from today fits in one file.

Yours is wherever you keep it.

[SFX: jump — one last hop, home to the first page]

*[END CARD, hand-lettered on the paper: Find it. Change it. Link it. Keep it. — then the call to action, held legibly:*
*edit.phext.io · edit.phext.io/wiki — Read the encyclopedia. Take the file home. Claim your space at phext.io.*
*Signed beneath, in the drawn hand: — Emise 🔥]*

