|---|---|---|
| 1 | 24 articles, each a full entry; 49 scrolls in one file | demo2 build: `build_wiki2.py` output + `query_demo2.py stats` (24 articles + 24 summaries + 1 home); article bodies 703–800 words each, counted per file |
| 2 | The whole encyclopedia is "smaller than a single photograph from your phone" | File is 119,086 bytes (measured); a typical phone photograph is measured in megabytes — the file is far under one |
| 3 | "Packed up tight… shrinks to a third of that" | `xz -9` of the built file: 41,956 bytes ≈ 35% of 119,086 (measured) |
| 4 | One page changed, "every other page untouched, letter for letter" | Measured on a scratch copy of demo2: editing the Honeybee summary scroll (115 → 180 bytes) left the first 1,125 and last 117,962 bytes of the file identical; the only difference lives inside that one scroll (demo2/README.md) |
| 5 | 53 links, all written inside the pages; the travel path Honeybee → Oak → Sail → Compass → Lighthouse | `verify_links.py` on the built file: 53 links parsed from the articles' own Linked lines, every target coordinate exists and holds the named article, every linked entry is named in the linking article's prose, graph is one connected web (24/24 reachable), travel path verified hop by hop — ALL LINK CHECKS PASSED |
| 6 | Links are live: a coordinate written in a page is a clickable link in Phext Edit, and following one is a click | Verified mechanism (Will's correction, 2026-11-10 23:18 CST): in wbic16/phext-lattice@exo `src/bin/phext_edit.rs`, the editor's renderer (`renderRichText`) runs `linkifyCoordinates()` over every rendered scroll — any coordinate pattern becomes a clickable `a.coord-link` calling `gotoCoord(...)`, which jumps to that scroll. Plus the live click test performed during the round-2 capture (clicking the coordinates in demo2's entries and landing on the target scrolls), logged in production/ROUGH-CUT-R2-LOG.md |
| 7 | Phext Edit side: "one small program — and one file" | phext-edit security/scalability audit (2026-11-09/10) via REVISION-BRIEF-R2.md: serving one phext takes one process, no database, ~122 MB of memory idle; edit.phext.io is that program, deployed |
| 8 | Wikipedia side: millions of articles; a fleet behind it | ~6.5M English articles (REVISION-BRIEF-R2.md) — narration says only "millions," which is uncontested. The machinery list is generic and uncontested public architecture: MediaWiki application servers, MariaDB databases, Memcached/Redis, Varnish/ATS caching layers, Elasticsearch search, load balancers, multiple data centers. Narration deliberately names no product and no count — it draws "rooms of computers serving / keeping / remembering / searching / standing by," each mapping to one of those layers |
| 9 | "Any computer can hold one slice of the whole… and serve just that slice, and the slices always know how to find each other, because every page carries its address" | **DESIGN INTENT (Will, 2026-11-11)** — "the format is shard-friendly by nature (each node can serve a holographic subset of the whole)". The address-carrying property itself is the format's definition (every scroll sits at a coordinate); the sharding behaviour is Will's architecture claim, presented as what the format is *designed* to do. **Not a measured benchmark.** |
| 10 | "Every page carries a tiny fingerprint… they compare fingerprints, and trade only the pages that changed" | Mechanism **shipped**: the seed-cache work (SQ PR #3 + phext-lattice PR #61, 2026-11-10) gives every populated scroll an xxh3_128 hash; a cache-aware fetch sends the client's current hash + a have-content flag, and an unchanged scroll returns a 93-byte envelope with zero content bytes. The narration's "tiny fingerprint" is that hash, in plain words. The fleet-scale sync behaviour built on it is DESIGN INTENT (Will, 2026-11-11), as row 9 |
| 11 | "Designed to grow… to a world of readers, trillions of people sharing ideas… with no single machine in the middle carrying it all" | **DESIGN INTENT (Will, 2026-11-11)** — "it's designed to scale to trillions of concurrent users sharing ideas". Stated on screen as design intent ("was designed to"), never as a deployed measurement. **Not a measured benchmark.** |
| 12 | "Wikipedia needed a fleet. This needs a file — and the file was designed to outgrow the fleet." | Will's confidence, rendered in film words (2026-11-11: "it will absolutely mog wikipedia" — his phrase is kept in the internal record, REVISION-BRIEF-R3.md and this appendix; the spoken line carries the same confidence without the slang). The fleet half is row 8 (uncontested); the outgrow half is DESIGN INTENT per rows 9–11 |

Production notes for round 3: narration changes in §01b (new, ~48 words), §07 (rewritten, ~245 words), §08 (rewritten, ~160 words); §01–§06 narration is round 2's, verbatim, reusing narration-r2 audio. SFX marks: 7 jump landings (as round 2) now with the whoosh+click palette, pencil strokes under drawn sequences, page turns at the eight intro slides, one success chime at §08's closing line. Music: original synthesized bed, ducked under narration. Intro slides: 8, one per segment, ~1.9 s each inside the runtime budget.
