# Revision Brief — Round 2 (Will, 2026-11-10 23:04 CST)

Will's direction, verbatim: "the animation should be artistic, bright white construction paper - hand-drawn effects in real-time, the script still needs many revisions:"

1. **Drop the Scroll Town call-out** — the Scrolltown/Fern ending is out entirely.
2. **Don't expect people to understand the technical details** — plain-language throughout; no assumed knowledge of databases, servers, bytes, or diffs. Show, don't explain.
3. **Focus on interlink** — show a demo of hyper-linking ideas *after they've been entered*: write entries first, then link them, then travel the links.
4. **Use real Wikipedia articles, or write full entries — not fake ones** — the current demo's 24 articles are short original stubs (titles are real topics: Honeybee, Oak, Octopus, …). Round 2 replaces them with full entries.
5. **Add sound effects for jumping between scrolls** — a designed jump sound, present in the mix.
6. **Animation: zoom out / projection / zoom in** — zoom out from one location, see a projection of the underlying complexity, then zoom into the target coordinate.
7. **Focus on the simplicity of it all** — including a side-by-side of the deployed tooling for Wikipedia versus Phext Edit.

## Standing fact for the script (verified 2026-11-10, phext-edit security/scalability audit)

The side-by-side's core image is the measured server stack, not a metaphor: serving ONE phext file takes one small program — about 122 MB of memory idle, one process, no database — versus Wikipedia's deployed fleet (MediaWiki/PHP application servers, MariaDB primaries + replicas, Memcached/Redis, Varnish/ATS caches, Elasticsearch, load balancers, deployed across multiple data centers). Wikipedia serves ~6.5M English articles (orders of magnitude more knowledge) — the contrast the film draws is *machinery per idea*, honestly framed: "for an encyclopedia you can hold, look how little machinery there is." The deployed edit.phext.io is itself the Phext Edit side of the shot — it is a real deployed tool, not a mockup.

## Interlink reality check — CORRECTED 2026-11-10 23:18 CST (Will; verified by Emise)

The original text of this section claimed Phext Edit has no hyperlink layer. **That was wrong.** Will corrected it ("Phext addresses are *automatically* hyperlinked"), and Emise verified against the shipped source before rewriting this section: in wbic16/phext-lattice@exo `src/bin/phext_edit.rs`, the editor's rich-text renderer (`renderRichText`) runs `linkifyCoordinates()` over every rendered scroll — any coordinate pattern in the text becomes a clickable `a.coord-link` that calls `gotoCoord(...)` and jumps to that scroll. Interlink is a shipped feature: entries link by writing each other's coordinates, and following a link is a click. The film demos real clicks; no prototype framing, no hedging.

## Style (supersedes the 21:56 round-1 style note)

Bright white construction paper; artistic; hand-drawn effects rendered in real time (drawn on as the narration proceeds — strokes appear, maps sketch themselves in). TED × Kurzgesagt clarity and simplicity remain; the paper is bright white, not warm/sepia.
