| 05 The unplugged test | 1:48.0–2:26.0 | Framed scroll through the file opened as plain text (byte-identical `.txt` copy rendered in the browser) → drawn size card: **119,086 bytes** (24 articles · 49 scrolls — smaller than one photograph) / packed **41,956 bytes** | Card rendered from the measured stats file of the rebuilt demo2 — see Correction note |
| 06 Ideas that point | 2:26.0–3:34.0 | Drawn page-fill (an entry being written) → framed Honeybee article capture → framed Linked-line close-up (the real rendered scroll, links visible) → drawn map: houses draw on → **the web draws itself (all 53 real threads)** → map hold → **TRAVEL, filmed as real clicks**: Honeybee→Oak [SFX], Oak→Sail [SFX], Sail→Compass [SFX], then **ZOOM SET-PIECE #2 (full form)**: pull back from Compass to the whole-map projection, drift, dive the thread into Lighthouse [SFX] → Lighthouse landing clip → closing map hold | Click segments are cut from the single verified travel take; hop 4's *filmed* click exists in the take, but the cut shows the zoom set-piece form for that hop per the script's stage direction — stated plainly here |
| 07 Two ways to build an encyclopedia | 3:34.0–4:22.0 | **Fully drawn side-by-side**: left, a pencil city grows element by element (serving / master copies / remembering / searching / stand-by copies in more than one place); right, one small program + one file draw — and the pencil stops; then the completed side-by-side holds | Narration's honest framing kept verbatim ("Nobody is saying the small one replaces the big one. It doesn't.") |
| 08 Your turn | 4:22.0–4:52.0 | Drawn fold-back (24 cards return into the one file) → framed home capture → closing hop home to the first page (Honeybee still) [SFX] → hand-lettered end card drawing on: "Find it. Change it. Link it. Keep it." + edit.phext.io, fade out | No Scrolltown, no Fern — per brief |

## SFX recipe (one jump sound, 7 placements)

Synthesised with ffmpeg sources only — no external samples.
`jump.wav`: an `aevalsrc` expression at 48 kHz, 0.5 s —
`0.55·sin(2π·(470t + 1050t²))·exp(−10t)` (a warm tonal blip sweeping
≈470 → ≈1,520 Hz with a fast exponential decay — the "landing")
plus `0.16·(random·2−1)·exp(−7t)` (a breath of white noise — the
paper whoosh), faded out over the last 0.12 s. Placed at the seven
landings listed in `narration-r2/TIMING.md` (45.41 / 65.50 / 183.90 /
186.90 / 188.50 / 194.67 / 282.90 s). A very gentle paper bed —
brown noise, low-passed at 420 Hz, at 0.012 gain (−38 dB class) —
runs under the whole film. Mix loudnormed two-pass to −14 LUFS.

## Capture facts

- All UI captures are Playwright `recordVideo` (1920×1080, page zoom
  150%, big cursor ring) against a **local phext-edit instance on
  aletheia-core** (`/home/wbic16/bin-new/phext-edit`, `--local`,
  loopback port 8902) serving **demo2's final file**. The live
  edit.phext.io (which serves the old demo/ stub) was **not** used
  for any UI shot. Chromium is the ranch's Playwright 1248 build,
  launched by explicit path (the installed Playwright 1.65 expects
  1254 — noted, not hidden).
- Terminal §04 is an asciinema cast (104×34, agg, Monokai, font 20)
  recorded on aletheia-core against a scratch copy in
  `r2/casts/demo2edit/`; demo2's pristine file was never modified
  (SHA-256 verified at wrap: `7a7a4be9…f564130`, unchanged).
- Assembly discipline matches previous cuts: segment encodes gated
  at ≤70 °C Tctl, 1080p30, CRF 18, loudnorm two-pass to −14 LUFS,
  faststart. Thermal facts: every segment encode started only after
  the gate read ≤70 °C (waits observed mid-build: 73.1 °C → 36.5 °C
  and 76.5 °C → 39.1 °C before starting; final runs gated at
  68.6 °C, 36.0 °C, 36.6 °C).

## Gaps and substitutions (stated plainly)

1. **§01 "browser closes" is drawn**, not a live capture: a pencil
   browser window is crossed out and the file opens locally. No
   live-site footage is used anywhere in this cut, by design (the
   live site serves the old stub demo).
2. **§06 hop 4**: the script's full-form zoom set-piece replaces the
   direct click visual for the Compass → Lighthouse hop in the cut.
   The click itself was filmed and verified in the travel take
   (click at 42.37 s, landing verified); the zoom is the scripted
   presentation of that same hop.
3. **§06 hops 2–3 are brisk** (3.0 s / 1.6 s windows, source sped
   ≈2.5×): the narration's travel lines are tightly packed
   (37.9 → 42.5 s), and the windows follow the words. Landings and
   SFX stay on the spoken marks.
4. **§04** remains the local terminal route (the proven route from
   rounds 1–2), not a signed-in editor edit take; the editor in
   `--local` mode can edit, but the byte-level before/after proof
   the script asks for is the terminal check shown.
5. **Zoom set-pieces are drawn-map animations** (crops of the
   3840-px drawn map), not screen recordings of a zooming UI —
   exactly as the brief's style law prescribes (the map is a drawn
   projection). The clicks that bookend them are real.
6. **Gate 1 (narration)** — Will's listen still gates release:
   pace, pauses, and pronunciations ("phext", "edit.phext.io").
7. **Production incident — opaque overlays (fixed, rebuilt).** The
   first full assembly rendered every paper-framed capture section
   black: the drawn-asset script's `save()` had converted PNGs to
   RGB, stripping alpha from the frame border and lower-third
   overlays, making them opaque black full frames. Caught by
   spot-frame viewing; overlays re-rendered as true RGBA and all
   affected segments rebuilt. The delivered file is the rebuilt one.
8. **Production incident — §05 first take filmed a 404 (re-shot).**
   The raw-text take ran before the byte-identical `.txt` copy of
   the file was created, so it recorded the browser's
   file-not-found page. Caught by spot-frame viewing; the take was
   re-shot against the real file (119,003 characters rendered;
   head of file verified) and §05 rebuilt.
9. **Cosmetic:** in §07, the "stand-by copies — in more than one
   place" label is partially overlapped by the second skyline's
   roofs. Text remains legible; left as-is for this cut.
